[Music]  the origins of  five common superstitions a black cat  crossing your path  cats have a long history as omens and  are long associated with magic  in ancient egypt they were seen as  harbingers of good luck  and as messengers for the gods their  association with bad luck however  started in medieval europe specifically  in the 14th century  this unfortunate belief became so bad  that during the black death  black cats were often hunted down and  killed in certain towns  as they were thought to be the  progenitors of the plague  in reality this was a case of mistaken  identity  cats were found in towns and cities with  plague outbreaks  because they were hunting an increased  rat population  we know now that these rats were the  true culprits of the plague  and most unfortunately the killing of  cats in these towns made the plague  worse  as the rat population would then run out  of control  cats also had an association with  witchcraft  the fear of witches reached its heights  in the 16th century and it was thought  that witches would sometimes take in  stray cats this caused people to believe  the cats especially black cats were  working with the witches  and doing their bidding or even that  cats were really witches in disguise  this assumption even came up in the  infamous salem witch trials  where owning a black cat was seen as  evidence against  a person suspected of witchcraft  interestingly in some cultures black  cats are seen as good luck charms  specifically in japan russia and some  parts of britain  breaking a mirror will cause seven years  of bad luck  mirrors like cats have had magical  associations for centuries  in some cultures people believed and in  some cases still do believe  that a mirror could steal a person's  soul this superstition originated in  classical rome  mirrors were thought to reflect or hold  a fragment of a person's soul  so when a mirror reflected your image  and was broken  it was thought to signify damage to the  soul as well as the glass  the original superstition was concerned  with natural cracks in a mirror  as mirrors back in roman times were  weaker and had more imperfections than  modern mirrors  so could break due to atmospheric  changes in the environment  the superstition was later expanded to  include accidental breaking as well  romans also believed that life went in  seven  year cycles so whatever luck you had  be it good or bad would be wiped away  every seven years  hence the seven years bad luck  walking under a ladder like some of the  other superstitions discussed  this partly has its origin in ancient  egypt  ladders were seen as pathways that gods  and human souls  would take to come and go from the  afterlife so egyptians were always sure  to be respectful  when dealing with ladders the modern  superstition though is christian in  origin  a ladder was a symbol of the holy  trinity the three sides make a triangle  the father the son and the holy ghost so  strolling under a ladder would be  breaking the trinity  and that would be bad indeed frankly  this superstition is probably the most  useful to follow  as objects and people often fall down  from ladders  knocking on wood knocking on wood is  often used to call good luck  or more specifically call off bad luck  it seems that the superstition has  indo-european and celtic roots  these groups believe that trees housed  spirits so knocking on wood would either  drive out bad spirits or call good  spirits for their protection  in christian folklore wood was seen to  symbolize the cross  so knocking on it would call the lord's  protection this meaning was probably an  adaptation  of the previous mentioned pagan  superstition  after the christianization of europe  throwing salt over your shoulder in  ancient and classical societies salt was  a very  valuable commodity so spilling salt was  a major waste of a valuable resource  and of money because of this it became  associated with bad fortune  throwing the spilled salt over your left  shoulder as an act of  penance was seen to negate this bad luck  in christian folklore the devil was  thought to be just over your left  shoulder  whispering lies and tempting you to sin  so throwing salt over it  would blind the devil and make him leave  you alone  also judas jesus's betrayer  was thought to have spilled salt at the  last supper and since  judas was a major villain in the bible  this  association stuck  [Music]  hello i really appreciate you checking  out my video  if you want to see more like this please  subscribe like the video if you liked it  leave a comment letting me know what you  think and check out some of my other  videos if you want to learn more  bye

